{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.OrthoschemeBaseIntegral","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.OrthoschemeBaseIntegral","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.LobachevskyDerivative","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.LobachevskyMaximum","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Integrals.LogTrigonometric"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_orthoscheme","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_orthoscheme","kind":"theorem","line":246,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L246-L326","doc":"**H4-4 (reduced) — the orthoscheme base integral is `½Λ(ψ)`:**\n\n    ∫₀^ψ −¼ · log( (cos²φ − cos²ψ) / cos²φ ) dφ  =  ½ Λ(ψ)   for 0 < ψ < π/2.\n\nThe integrand is Milnor's angular integrand for the right triangle with angle\n`ψ` at the circumcentre, after the elementary radial integration.  It is\nunbounded at `φ = ψ` (where the base vertex meets the circle and the fibre mass\ndiverges — `fibreMass_eq_top`), so the identity is a genuine improper-integral\nstatement, and the integrability that makes it meaningful is\n`intervalIntegrable_log_two_sin`, not continuity.\n\n`ψ < π/2` is Milnor's acute hypothesis in local form: it is what puts `cos ψ > 0`\nand therefore the foot of the circumcentre perpendicular at positive distance on\nthe correct side of the edge. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integrableOn_orthoscheme","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integrableOn_orthoscheme","kind":"theorem","line":223,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L223-L244","doc":"**The orthoscheme integrand is integrable on the open interval `(0, ψ)`.**\n\nStated for `Ioo` rather than `Icc`/`Ioc` on purpose: the three-log decomposition\nholds pointwise only where `φ < ψ` strictly, and `φ = ψ` is where the integrand\nblows up.  This is what makes the improper integral in `integral_orthoscheme` a\nreal number rather than Lean's junk value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_log_sin_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_log_sin_sub","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L218-L221","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_log_sin_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_log_sin_add","kind":"theorem","line":213,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L213-L216","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.log_orthoscheme_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.log_orthoscheme_eq","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L186-L207","doc":"**The three-log decomposition of the orthoscheme integrand**, valid pointwise\non the OPEN interval `0 ≤ φ < ψ < π/2`.\n\nIt fails at `φ = ψ` — there the left side is `log 0 = 0` by Lean's convention\nwhile the right side is not — which is exactly why the integral identity below\nneeds an `a.e.` rewrite and why the integrability statement is about `Ioo`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.cos_sq_sub_cos_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.cos_sq_sub_cos_sq","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L173-L179","doc":"`cos²φ − cos²ψ = sin(ψ+φ)·sin(ψ−φ)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_log_sin_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_log_sin_eq","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L149-L166","doc":"The `log sin` form, with the `log 2` from the normalization made explicit.\nThis is the shape the orthoscheme computation consumes three times. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.Lob_eq_integral","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.Lob_eq_integral","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L141-L147","doc":"**`Λ(x) = −∫₀ˣ log(2 sin t) dt` on `[0, π]`** — the classical definition of\nthe Lobachevsky function, recovered as a theorem from the `Cl₂` series\ndefinition rather than assumed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_neg_log_two_sin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.integral_neg_log_two_sin","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L126-L139","doc":"**`Λ` is the primitive of `−log(2 sin)`:**\n\n    ∫_a^b −log(2 sin t) dt = Λ(b) − Λ(a)   for  0 ≤ a ≤ b ≤ π.\n\nThis is the integral form of T1's `hasDerivAt_Lob`, and the form in which `Λ` is\nclassically *defined*.  The endpoints are included: `Λ'` blows up at `0` and `π`,\nso the fundamental theorem is used with the derivative required only on the open\ninterval, and the integrability across the singularity is\n`intervalIntegrable_log_two_sin`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_log_two_sin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.intervalIntegrable_log_two_sin","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L112-L124","doc":"`log (2 sin ·)` is interval-integrable on ANY interval.\n\nThe log singularities at the zeros of `sin` are integrable; Mathlib proves this\nfor `log ∘ sin` (`intervalIntegrable_log_sin`) with no hypothesis on the\nendpoints, and the constant `log 2` splits off almost everywhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.ae_sin_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.ae_sin_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L105-L110","doc":"Almost every real has `sin t ≠ 0`.  This is what lets `log (2 sin t)` be\nsplit as `log 2 + log (sin t)` under an integral, even though the splitting fails\npointwise exactly where `sin` vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.measure_sin_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicVolume.measure_sin_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrthoschemeBaseIntegral.lean#L97-L103","doc":"The zero set of `sin` is null — it is contained in the countable set `πℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}